#!/bin/bash
#
# qlib -- Debugging and execution framework for bash scripts.
#       Attempts to make life easy for the developer.
# Author: Ted Merrill, March 2023   Updated: 2023-04-03
#
##############################################################################
# Q FEATURES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# -- Features are generally optional.
#       But for full advantage, follow all Q programming guidelines.
# -- Parameter passing / value return support :
#       Makes it simpler to pass values between caller and caller
#       functions, avoiding some bash horrors.
# -- Standardized message generation, both user and debug.
#       Makes it easier to understand the messages.
#       Debug statements are simple and generally can be left it.
# -- Standardized error handling.
# -- Standarized user interaction.
# -- Interactive debugger
# -- Breakpoint debug method, with or without debugger.
# -- Any program properly written with Q is also a library
#       That is, you can source it into another script and call its
#       functions; the script at the top level (that invoked the
#       other script(s)) decides which function runs first
#       (or you can drop into the debugger).
# -- You can avoid learning so much about the horrors of bash if
#       you stick to the subset of bash recommended.
#       In spite of bash's problems, it is a useful language when
#       you can solve a problem by tying together a number of
#       external programs.
#
#
# Q CAVEATS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# -- To gain all the advantages, you must do some "mark up" of your
#       code, and do it consistenly. Not hard, but perhaps not suitable
#       for a large existing bash program.
# -- If you are worried about efficiency, you would not use bash,
#       but Q certainly makes it a little worse.
# -- Bash is a quirky, unsafe language. Cannot entirely hide that.
#
#
# GETTING STARTED
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# -- Read through this file, perhaps skipping everything inside of a function;
#       look for the descriptions of how to use the functions.
# -- Read through qtemplate. Follow instructions therein and experiment.
#       (qtemplate is an example bash program you can copy and modify)
# -- Read through this file again.
# -- Do NOT use the functions in this file as much of an example of how
#       to write your code; most of these functions attempt to hide the
#       messiness of using bash from you, and as a result are messy themselves.
#
#
# BASH VARIABLE NAME SCOPING
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Bash has horrendous issues with name scoping.
# With bash, variables never need to be declared.
# If you take no care, all variables are "global" and it is more than
# easy for a called function to trash the variables in use by a caller
# function!
#
# For obvious(?) reasons, name all variables intended to be global
# with names that are likely to be unique.
# This is usually accomplished by staking out certain name prefixes
# with the hope that noone else will use them.
# For example, qlib hopes that no other code will use variables (or functions)
# beginning with "q".
# A good bet is to pick a 3 or 4 letter prefix that is unlikely to be
# the beginning of any English word.
# This applies to function names as well as global variables.
# For example, you might name your project "zok" and and make the names
# of all the global variables, functions and even the filenames begin with
# "zok".
#
# Mistyping easily causes access to variable names that were never
# intended to exist. Values assigned disappear into nothingness.
# By default, qlib sets the bash -u flag (e.g. set -u).
# This causes reads from unset variables to abort the entire program.
# (Writing to an unset variable makes it "set" so it is only one way).
# Unfortunately, a violation of -u is punished by aborting the program.
# It is sad that the entire program aborts, but perhaps it is worth it.
# If not, then add to your script (following source ./qlib and before
# invoking qStart) the line:
#       set +u   # Disable undefined variable fatal errors
#
# Minimize variables declared outside of any function.
# Have the barest minimum amount of code outside of any function.
# 
# Declare almost all your variables with the bash "local" construct.
# This protects any variable of the same name deeper in the calling stack;
# the deeper variable cannot be accessed at all.
# Please note this does NOT protect the variables of the function from
# being thrashed by other functions called by the function.
# All functions must be written to protect their callers by using "local".
# Do this consistently.
#
# If your function accepts a variable name as an argument, so that it can
# be written to (or read?) then your function must use unique names for its
# local variables, so as to not hide the variable of the caller;
# and hope that the caller will never use variable names that could collide
# with those in the called function.
# Try to avoid writing this type of code, since qlib already supports you.
# Several qlib functions accept variable names as arguments, and they
# are hopefully as safe as possible in how they work.
# Note that the only way to write a value whose name was passed is via
# the bash builtin printf function with the -v option (or perhaps read -v).
#
#
# THE SUBSET OF BASH TO USE IF YOU ARE NOT A BASH EXPERT
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# See above about variable name scoping, and stick to it,
# and below about using Q features.
# Besides that...
#
# Use double qoutes around variable dereferences, e.g.
#       "$var"
# if there is the slightest possiblity that the variable might contain
# any characters special to bash.
# It would be sane to always do this double quoting.
#
# Avoid using the && , || and { and } operators except for the occasional 
# very simple case, as the syntax and logic are hard to get right.
# Instead use if statement ; then statement; statement ; fi ...
# the syntax is still difficult, but the logic is pretty easy.
# The remaining logic problem is that bash has truth/false backwards from
# the civilized world.
# You will end up putting a lot of ! operators into your code, e.g.
#       if ! mycommand myarg ; then
#           errorhandler 
#           qReturnFail "big doo doo"; return 1
#       fi
#       do what we need to do 
# Use indentation to make nesting clear.
#
# For testing variables and related, use the bash "test" builtin command.
# This makes your code easy to read and understand.
#
# Always make sure positional arguments exist before using them.
# After testing, copy them into a local variable and shift immediately.
# Use the following test, e.g.:
#       if test $# -gt 0 ; then
#           local arg="$1" ; shift
#           do something with $arg
#       fi
# Try to avoid needing to rewind fetching arguments, but here is how to do it:
#       set -- "$arg" "$@"   # push back fetched arg
#
#
# GLOBAL CONFIGURATION VARIABLES
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Unix (Linux) and bash have specific support for passing key=value pairs
# between programs; these are called environmental variables.
# Bash extends this within a bash script with "key" being a variable name
# which is given the value.
# All such environmental variables are automatically bash variables.
# While this has much opportunity for abuse, it is also very handy.
#
# Each layer of programs and scripts and functions within your program
# can have the opportunity to override or supplement the value passed to it.
# The usual case is to have each script provide a default value in case
# one was not passed.
# As a shortcut, it is usually assumed that an empty value is equivalent
# to not having been passed. (But beware of accessing unset variables in a
# bash script (see above about set -u).
# Try to make it so that configuration variables never need an empty value
# (i.e. so that at some level, perhaps the last, a non-empty value will
# be substituted).
#
# Q strongly encourages the following at the main level of your scripts
# (see qtemplate for more information) e.g.:
#     : ${foobarConfigExample:='configuration-example'}
# In this example, foobarConfigExample is given a value only if it was not
# set or if it had an empty value.
# This allows setting this from a parent script or from the interactive
# command line... so do this even for the most lowly script.
#
# Example: This turns on debugging messages:
#       qbug=y ./qlib command arg...
# This turns on breakpoints:
#       qbp=y ./qlib command arg...
# This does both:
#       qbug=y qbp=y ./qlib command arg...
# 
#
#
# USE OF stdin, stdout and stderr
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# stdin: qReadUser reads from stdin. You could certainly use bash read function
# directly; qReadUser gives a standardized experience to it which may
# be appropriate for commandline interaction with a user.
#
# stderr: qlib always writes messages to stderr, except where the message is a
# return or value (see next paragraph).
# qlib provides qprint as the alternative to echo, and qprintf as the alternative
# to printf.
#
# stdout: A few qlib functions have the option of (or always) emit to stdout
# as a way of returning values. And a script can be written (as qlib is) so that
# if it is the top level script, it will execute a command/function passed
# as argument(s) and the write the result (with added newline) to stdout. 
# To write to stdout, typically you would use bash builtins echo or printf.
#
#
# CALLING FUNCTIONS, PASSING OPTIONS / ARGUMENTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Pass arguments to functions in the usual bash way, and usually call them
# in the usual bash way.
#
# You may sometimes want to precede the call to the function with qrun,
# which provides various control and debugging capabilies;
# this is particularly helpful when calling higher level functions,
# and provides a way to get results back (describe below).
# For example,
#       local myvariable
#       if ! qrun r=myvariable myfunction myargs ; then
#           qReturnFail "Couldnt do it" ; return 1
#       fi
#       if test -z "$myvariable" ; then
#            something
#       fi
# qrun has options for retrying, and receiving results from the commands
# stdout.
#
#
# RECEIVING OPTIONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Q encourages the following style of programming of functions in regards
# to receiving of options by functions. 
# You can do whatever you want, but this style is easiest,
# most extensible and provides nice consistency:
#
# Your function should almost always begin with:
#       qEnter "$@"
# This adds automatic debugging capability.
# If you omit it, you lose that capability.
# You may want to omit it for very low level functions.
# 
# Options should proceed positional parameters.
# Positional parameters should be avoided if there are, or will possibly be,
# many distinct parameters to pass.
# The obvious time to have positional parameters is for a variable length
# list of parameters (e.g. a list of filenames or etc.), which would be
# at the end of the arguments to the function.
#
# Options should be allowed in any order; delay processing of the options
# until all have been accumulated.
# The Q function qOption can then be used with a minimum of coding,
# particulary if your option names are the same as the variable names holding
# the result.
#
# It is preferrable to use the following form for options:
#       key=value
# This avoids confusion about which option and which value go together.
# Avoid beginning options with '-' as such decoration is entirely 
# unnecessary and just adds to the general clutter.
#
#
# RETURNING SUCCESS/FAILURE STATUS FROM FUNCTIONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# Success/failure is returned by the bash return statement in it's usual
# backward-ness : return 0 for success, return 1 (or nonzero) for failure.]
#
# To allow for debugging, always precede each "return 1" with a call to
# qReturnFail.  You may pass a debugging message to qReturnFail, e.g.
#       qReturnFail "we died, sorry" ; return 1
#
# Likewise, for debugging always precede each "return 0" with a call to
# qReturnOk.  You may pass a return result at the same time, e.g.
#       qReturOk "$result" ; return 0
#
#
# RECEIVING SUCCESS/FAILURE STATUS FROM FUNCTIONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# It should rarely or never be necessary to use $? ...
# use $? only with great care as bash quickly loses it.
# This should be tested# with the usual backward if or while bask constructs.
#
# By default, qlib sets the bash -e flag (e.g. set -e).
# This causes unhandled errors (i.e. unhandled nonzero return values)
# to fault the calling function.
# This is useful information about a possible bug in your program,
# but sometimes may be spurious.
# At least it is only the calling function, and not the entire program.
# To deliberately throw away the return status, try:
#       cmd || true
#
# Note that qrun returns the status of the command it executed
# (adding debugging).
#
# 
# RETURNING VALUES FROM FUNCTIONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# To pass back actual return data, you may invent your own methods, but
# Q specifically provides a method that simplify your coding
# and provides debugging and breakpointing.
#
# Call qReturnOk with the result as an argument (with no argument,
# the result is empty, which often makes sense).
# Do this just before the return 0 statement, e.g.
#       qReturnOk "$result" ; return 0
#
# Under the hood, qReturnOk puts the result in the global variable qresult.
# Usually you should avoid every referencing qresult directly...
# it should work, but you lose debugging capability.
#
# Another method is to simply print the result to stdout (e.g. using echo).
# However, it is rare that your own functions would do this with each other
# as there are various practical problems.
#
#
# RECEIVING VALUES FROM CALLED COMMANDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# qrun r=varname command...
# The command is executed and the return value is placed into the variable
# named varname.
# Options to qrun determine how the result is returned from "command" to
# qrun.
# The default method is that the called command is a function in your
# program that calls qReturnOk to pass the return value back.
#
# With an option, qrun will read the stdout of the command and pass it
# back to you; this is typically necessary where the command is an external
# program.
# NOTE: with this option, the command is run in a subshell and it is
# impossible for it to modify variables in your program.
# Therefore, this method should probably not be used with your own functions.
#
# qResult varname
# You don't need qrun everywhere. It's fine to call your own functions
# directly.  The function qResult, called with a variable name
# will put the result into the named variable, e.g.:
#       if ! somefunction someargs ; then qReturnFail ; return 1 ; fi
#       local myresult; qResult myresult
#
# Under the hood, qrun and qReuslt get the result via 
# the global variable qresult.
# Usually you should avoid every referencing qresult directly...
# it should work, but you lose debugging capability.
# Both qResult and qrun clear qresult after returning it's value,
# which avoids certain problems that can arise.
#
# In some places it makes sense to use $qresult directly.
# An example is qOption which returns the number of arguments it uses, 
# for example (in qtemplate):
#     qOption 'filepath,maxwidgets,maxsize,'  ; shift $qresult
#
# With no arguments, qResult writes the result (passed by qReturnOk)
# to the stdout. This allows the following, e.g.:
#       local myresult=$(qResult)
# This can be very quirky since qResult is running in a subshell;
# therefore it should be avoided.
# 
# USER INTERACTION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# qlib provides qUserRead which prompts the user and then accepts
# an answer.
# Or you can print the prompt directly using qUserPrompt and then
# do your own reading of the input if you really want.
# See comments above qUserPrompt and qUserRead (below).
# 
#
# MESSAGES
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# qlib emits various messages for command line use for
# developer and user information, using the following.
# The messages may be suppressed according to class of variable
# by various flags, esp. configuration variable qbug=y turns on
# debug level messages.
# The messages are colored (e.g. error are red) unless turned off
# with configuration variable qprettydiag=n .
#
# qerror message...
#       This is for a message that indicate to the user a serious
#       error leading to failure.
#       Prints message to stderr only if global variable qerror is set to y
#       (the default).
#       This also a breakpoint if interactive debugging.
# 
# qstatus message...
#       This is for a message that indicate to the user a routine condition
#       for the user's education or so they know that your program is 
#       actually doing something.
#       Prints message to stderr only if global variable quser is set to y
#       (the default).
#       Messages are not colored.
#       
# qbug message...
#       This is for a message to aid a developer in debugging the program.
#       A normal user would not want to see such messages.
#       Prints message to stderr only if global variable qbug is set to y
#       (NOT the default).
#
# The messages generate include function name and line number, which
# is sufficient to let you know that execution has reached your statement.
# Therefore, you can often simply omit the message.
# This includes qerror calls for conditions deemed unlikely and/or
# difficult to describe to a user.
#
# In addition, qEnter, qReturnOk, qReturnFail and qResult all have their
# own flavor of messages, but all only if qbug=y (i.e. for developers).
#
# qUserPrompt can be used to print a prompt to stderr that would be 
# appropriate before reading user input.
# It is called automatically by qUserRead (unless turned off by options)
# and so probably need not be called directly.
#
#
# TRAPPING EXCEPTIONS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The configuration variable qtrap may be set to y to enable trapping
# on exceptions that would otherwise kill the program immediately.
# (By default, it is on; set to n to disable trapping).
# It is not in general possible to resume execution of the program
# from where it was so the best policy is to look around, clean up,
# and exit the program.
# This a limitation of bash.
#
# When an exception is trapped, a message is printed and then a 
# back trace of the call stack.
# If breakpoints are enabled (qbp=y) the interactive debugger will
# be entered, from where you can inspect variables and even run some
# parts of your program.
# After this, application trap handlers will be called, if any.
#
# Applications may provide trap handlers to do cleanups before the
# program exits due to an exception.
# Such trap handlers are called with no arguments and should return 0.
# They can do all normal sort of stuff, subject to that the program
# may be in an unknown state.
# Applications should add their trap handlers by placing a call in the
# initialization code, after source'ing other files and 
# before the call to qStart (best, just before):
#       source ...
#       ...
#       qTrapHandlerAdd myTrapHandler
#       ...
#       qStart ....
# Such trap handlers are called in the order added, which should work out
# to be that lower level handlers are called first.
#
#
# DECLARING BREAKPOINTS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Q implements breakpoints only where there is an explicit command:
#       qbreakpoint
# You may add these anywhere without harm (they only do something
# when enabled), but typically you would only add these in a few
# places as having too many slows down the developer doing debugging,
# and they may be unnecessary if the breakpoints included in 
# qlib routines are sufficient.
#
# qlib has breakpoints (qbreakpoint) in key places such as qEnter
# and especially qReturnOk and qReturnFail.
# These breakpoints are only reached when qbpq=y (default is n).
# You can enable them by setting qbpq=y.
# With qbp=y qbpq=n , only breakpoints you add will be reached,
# plus a breakpoint on exception handling is always reached.
#
# When breakpoints are enabled (by setting qbp=y) the debugger
# will be invoked (default action, which can be changed) at every
# qbreakpoint.
# Fortunately, the debugger allows disabling breakpoints as they
# are encountered, so in a debugging session you can quickly disable
# the individual breakpoints you are not interested in.
#
# See the discussion below about debugging using breakpoints.
#
#
# BASIC Q USAGE AT SCRIPT LEVEL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Best bet is to copy qtemplate to a name of your choosing, follow the
# instructions therein, and modify your copy to do something useful.
# Following is an overview:
#
# Begin your script with:
#       #!/bin/bash
# ... because this is required to be the very first line for
# proper operation if the script is marked executable and executed
# by it's name or filepath.
#
# Then, for safety, abort on unhandled errors:
#       set -e
# and detect commad failures:
#       set -o pipefail
# This can be extended to detection of use of undefined variables
# by the line:
#       set -u
# Note that the 'u' set option may bite particularly hard as variables
# must be set to something (perhaps empty) before using, even
# positional parameters such as $1!
# And failure means the program aborts.
#
# Next, since your script might be sourced into another script, include
# a line like:
#       if test -z "$myOnce" ; then myOnce=y ;  else return 0 ; fi
# ... where "my" is a name you use uniquely.
# This will prevent accidental double-sourcing of your script.
#
# Next, if you want to override defaults for any qbug configuration
# variables, put that here, for example:
#       : ${qbug:=y}  # Make default debug choice be yes
# The above form allows overriding this choice from environmental
# variables for example from the terminal command line or
# another script:
#       qbug=n myscript
# ... where myscript is your script that uses qlib.
#
# Importantly, bring in qlib! :
#       source ./qlib || { echo MISSING qlib 2>&1 ; exit 1; }
# ... assuming that qlib is to be found in your current directory.
# Q encourages running a program with all components in the same
# directory.
#
# You may also use 
#       source qlib
# but in this case qlib will be searched for first in all the
# directories named in the environmental variable PATH (qlib does
# NOT need to be marked executable) and only if not found there
# it will be located in the current directory.
# 
#
# BASIC Q USAGE IN BASH SUBROUTINES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# [Note: Bash documentation uses the name "function" instead 
#  of "subroutine"; I will use them inter-changeably]
# 
# qEnter:
# Each subroutine should begin with the command
#       qEnter [<msg>]...
# Most usually and usefully, this is
#       qEnter "$@"
# The arguments of qEnter are used for diagnostics.
# qEnter, when debugging messages are enabled, prints the
# message (e.g. the list of arguments passed) in a debugging
# message, very useful for debugging!
# It also clears qresult to avoid confusion (see below).
#
# qReturnFail, qReturnOk:
# Every bash subroutine should ideally return to the caller, 
# in all cases, including at the end of the subroutine,
# with one of the following:
#       qReturnOk [<returnvalue>]... ; return 0
#       qReturnFail [<diagnostic-msg>]... ; return 1
# [Sadly there seems to be no way to get these routines to return
# to caller for you... too bad! Be sure to do it yourself!].
# These two routines print diagnostics when enabled.
# More importantly, qReturnOk joins its arguments (with spaces)
# and puts the return value where it can be obtained by the caller,
# a variable called qresult.
#
# Note: Failure to call these routines (e.g. omitting a qEnter or
# returning without calling qReturnOk or qReturnFail) is unlikely
# to cause any big problems, so long as the return result gets
# back somehow. But using them has big advantages.
#
# If you don't want to use qReturnOk/qReturnFail, you have a few
# options, none of them great:
#     bash functions, as well as Linux programs in general, do directly
# return small integers besides the usual 0 for success 
# ("true" for bash) and 1 for failure ("false" for bash.
# The bash variable $? holds the latest result.
# Since only small integers are supported, this is pretty limited.
#     Your function can emit text to stdout (e.g. printf) where it
# can be captured by a caller in the form of result=$(cmd args).
# This is a very nice and concise approach that, however, suffers from
# a few serious limitations. The function is executed in a separate
# process so no "side effects" of the function (such as setting "global"
# variables) have any effect for the caller... sad!
# Another common problem is that there is easily confusion resulting
# in other things besides the result being printed to stdout.
#     Your function can values in "global" variables. In fact this is
# what qReturnOk, qReturnFail and some other Q functions do.
# So long as the "global" variables are uniquely named, this works well,
# provided that the caller immediately stores the result(s) in a safe
# place so as to not be overwritten by another call to your function!
# [NOTE: bash doesn't actually have "global" variables. All variables
# that are set in the main context or in a function are inherited by child
# function calls, unless hidden by a local declaration in a function.
# You can do some interesting things with declaring global variables as
# local, but this must be considered vary carefully.]
#     Your function can be passed the name of a variable to put the
# return value into. The only way to do this is via the bash builtin
# read or printf -v commands; 
# you can't do e.g. $$varname=value which won't do that.
# You also have to be very careful that the variable name passed has not
# been shadowed by a local variable of your own; so you must give all your
# local variables very unique names.
# Because of these difficulties, it is recommended that you use 
# qrun r=<varname> <cmd>...
# ... and avoid writing your own code to return values like this.
#
#
# DEBUGGING USING MESSAGES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# You can see all of the messages from all of the qbug commands
# in your script by invoking your script with e.g.:
#       qbug=y ./myprogram
# This includes all of the qbug's in qlib functions, especially
# qEnter, qReturnOk, qReturnFail, qrun and qResult ... which
# gives you a lot of information about what your program is doing.
# You can add additional qbug commands whereever it seems useful.
#
# If you are overwhelmed by the volume of debug messages, you might
# temporarily put the following just before a key section:
#       local qbug=y   
# and just after that section:
#       local qbug=n
# Just remember to take these out when done!
# The "local" will provide cheap protection for the rest of the code
# to run with qbug unmolested.
#
#
# DEBUGGING USING DIRECT FUNCTION ACCESS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# If your code is in a fle of the "library" type you can directly call
# any subroutine from your command line, e.g.
#       ./myprogram myInnerFunction myarg1 myarg2
# A "library" file is distinguished by ending with:
#       qStart - "$@"
#
# If your file is of the "program", meaning it ends with
#       qStart myHighLevelFunction "$@"
# then you can write a very small test file, say "mytest", like:
#       #!/bin/bash
#       : ${qbug:=y}   # set debugging parameters here
#       source ./myprogram
#       qStart - "$@"
# and run it as (if you mark the file executable)
#       ./mytest myInnerFunction myarg1 myarg2
# or:
#       bash mytest myInnerFunction myarg1 myarg2
#
# You can systematically call your inner functions and test them.
# It may help sometimes to add "unit test" functions specifically to
# aid in testing inner functions; you can probably leave these in
# permanently.
#
#
# DEBUGGING USING BREAKPOINTS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# You can activate all of the breakpoints in your scripts with e.g.:
#       qbp=y ./myprogram
# By default, this will bring up the debugger (qDebuggerI) at each
# breakpoint (including ones built into qlib).
# [The default breakpoint action may be modified by overriding the
# configuration variable qbpoint_defaultcmd ... an expert option].
#
# qlib has breakpoints (qbreakpoint) in key places such as qEnter
# and especially qReturnOk and qReturnFail.
# These breakpoints are only reached when qbpq=y (the default).
# You can disable them by setting qbpq=n.
# By doing so, you will skip all qlib breakpoints and keep going
# until you reach a breakpoint that you added to your code that
# uses qlib.
#
# Assuming the defaults (apart from setting qbp=y), 
# you will enter the debugger and see the prompt QDBUG.
# See below about general use of the debugger before using it.
#
# You can add breakpoints to your code by simply adding a line
#       qbreakpoint
# In this case you may want to turn off the qlib builtin breakpoints.
# A great way to do this is to write your own simple script:
#       #!/bin/bash
#       : ${qbug:=y}    # y : turn on debug messages (mabye?)
#       : ${qbp:=y}     # y : turn on breakpoints
#       : ${qbpq:=n}    # n : disable qlib builtin breakpoints
#       source ./qlib
#       qStart mymainfunction "$@"
# If this simple script is called "mytest", you can then run it:
#       bash mytest
# and your program will fly by.
# If you see a problem (e.g. from the debug messages), locate a point
# in your code and add a breakpoint, then rerun mytest.
# Your code will enter the debugger when it reaches your breakpoint.
# (If it reaches some other breakpoint, enter B to disable that
# breakpoint, then c to continue).
# When it reaches your breakpoint, you can look at the values of
# the variables, e.g.:
#       echo $myvariable
# When you are done with debugging that, you might remove the 
# qbreakpoint command from your code, although you could leave it in.
#
# Note that the debugger learns about breakpoints only as they are 
# entered. Therefore it is necessary to enter each breakpoint once
# at least before you will be able to disable it specifically.
# The currently reached breakpoint can be disabled with the "B"
# command (use "h" for help)... henceforth hitting that breakpoint
# will execute an alternate command, by default the one given by the
# configuration variable qbpoint_altcmd which merely prints information
# instead of entering the debugger.
#
# For a more fine tuned approach to disabling breakpoints, you can
# use the "b" (lower case b) command to disable the current breakpath.
# A breakpath is whole call stack sequence leading up to and including
# the breakpoint.
# Henceforth, the breakpoint is disabled only for that exact breakpath.
#
# So in general, each time you run your program using the debugger,
# you will have to train the debugger which breakpoints to ignore.
# Normally this is not a painful process, as you just use b or B commands
# to disable unwanted breakpoints and then continue.
#
# Specific to breakpoints, the debugger has other commands:
# l -- list breakpaths encountered so far, and their associated command.
# L -- list breakpoints encountered so far, and their associated command.
# e -- dialog to edit breakpath entries
# E -- dialog to edit breakpoint entries
# 
# Each breakpoint and breakpath has an associated command.
# Each such command begins with the letter y or n which specifies
# whether the breakpoint/breakpath is enabled (y) or disabled.
# Following the y or n may be an optional subcommand; this is an 
# advanced feature for which you should refer to the qbreakpoint code.
#
# Note: the debugger only works if program was started with qStart,
# which initializes the debugger global variables appropriately.
#
#
# DEBUGGING USING INTERACIVE DEBUGGER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# First you need to get into the debugger.
#
# Your program will go into the debugger immediately if it begins work 
# with something like:
#       qStart - "$@"
#
# Otherwise, you can just start your program with breakpoints enabled,
# e.g.:
#       qbp=y ./myprogram myarg1 myarg2
# This will quickly bring you to the debugger.
#
# You can turn breakpoints off once in the debugger, if you want:
#       qbp=n
#
# The debugger accepts single line bash scripts of any sort.
# It is easy to crash your program or mangle the program state.
# You can call any of your functions and set any variables in scope.
#
# To continue the normal execution of your program, just press ENTER
# for the default "c" command  (c = continue).
#
# Enter "h" for a short help message that explains the single letter
# commands built in to the debugger.
# Most of these regard breakpoints, see the above discussion on 
# breakpoints.
# 
# Anything input line you enter that does not match the builtin single letter
# commands is passed to the bash "eval" command and is interpreted as a
# shell script.
# Here are some examples of useful things you can do:
#       set +u    -- turn off undefined variable checking 
#       qbug=y    -- turn on debug messages
#       qbpq=n    -- turn off qlib breakpoints
#
# You can call any function in your program or any external command. 
